// Practice quiz: Keeping sensitive data out of prompts
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. First draft, not reviewed. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/data-pipelines/keeping-sensitive-data-out-of-prompts

::o1 secret::Which value is a secret that must never go into a prompt? {
~A product category name #Not quite. A category names no person and grants no access.
~A random reference generated for this request #Not quite. It carries no access and maps back only on your side.
~A count of records #Not quite. A count reveals no identity or access.
=An API token #Correct. A token grants access to a system, so exposing it is a security incident.
}

::o1 free text::A record has no email field, but a free-text message says "call me on my mobile". Why is it still a concern? {
~Free text is always safe #Not quite. People write personal details into free text all the time.
~Models cannot read free text #Not quite. Reading free text is usually the point.
=Free text can contain personal data that field checks miss #Correct. Reviewing column names alone is not enough, so messages need masking or review.
~Only numbers can be personal data #Not quite. Names and addresses are personal data too.
}

::o2 order::What should you try first when minimizing a record for a prompt? {
~Mask every field and send them all #Not quite. Masking is for what you must keep; dropping comes first.
=Drop every field the task does not need #Correct. A field never sent cannot leak.
~Encrypt the prompt yourself #Not quite. The service would need to read it, so this does not minimize anything.
~Ask the model to forget the data #Not quite. Instructions in a prompt do not remove data already sent.
}

::o2 pseudonym::What does pseudonymizing an id with a random token let you do? {
~Send the identity safely in another field #Not quite. The aim is not to send it at all.
~Stop protecting the mapping #Not quite. The mapping is sensitive and must be protected.
~Skip reviewing the free text #Not quite. Free text can still hold personal data.
=Join the result back to the record, with the mapping kept on your side #Correct. The service sees only a token that means nothing without your mapping.
}

::o3 log::Which log line is safest for a failed enrichment? {
=The reference and the exception class name #Correct. This supports debugging with no personal data or unbounded text.
~The full record, so debugging is easy #Not quite. This copies personal data into every log destination.
~The full prompt, since it was minimized #Not quite. Prompts should still stay out of logs by default.
~The API token, to show which account failed #Not quite. Secrets must never be logged.
}
